---
// Measure AI search referrals and citations: checked against the analytics,
// page feedback, search analytics, assistant analytics, metadata, sitemap,
// and deployment docs, and against packages/blume/src/analytics (script,
// google-analytics, head), components/layout (Analytics.astro,
// analytics-client.ts, page-feedback.ts, search/analytics.ts), and the
// seo.metatags schema on main. Third-party facts checked against Google's AI
// features page (updated 2025-12-10), the Search Console Performance and
// verification help, the GA4 default channel group, traffic acquisition,
// key events, and custom dimension help, OpenAI's publishers FAQ, and Bing's
// AI Performance posts (2026-02-10 and 2026-06-16). Ran: the blume.config.ts
// below parsed with blumeConfigSchema, and the app_click script in happy-dom
// (fires once per click on an app link, ignores other links, and a second
// run adds no second listener). Not yet run end to end on a deployed site.
// Review pass: re-checked GA4's key-event steps and AI Assistant channel rule,
// and Bing's AI Performance wording (surfaces, sampled grounding queries).
// 2026-10-05: added Search Console's Generative AI performance report (rolled
// out to every site 2026-08-31), checked against its help page and an export
// of the report for useblume.dev.
import CodeBlock from "blume/components/content/CodeBlock.astro";
import GuidePage from "../_guides/GuidePage.astro";
import { guideById } from "../_guides/guides.ts";
---

<GuidePage guide={guideById("measure-ai-search-documentation")}>
  <p>
    You can see part of it, but no single report adds it up. Citations,
    referrals, and what readers do next are separate numbers from separate
    tools. Bing Webmaster Tools counts how often Copilot and Bing's AI answers
    cite your pages. Your analytics shows readers who clicked through from an
    assistant, and ChatGPT tags its links with{" "}
    <code>utm_source=chatgpt.com</code>. Search Console counts how often AI
    Overviews and AI Mode showed a link to your pages, but their clicks stay
    inside its ordinary Web totals. None of them proves anyone adopted your
    product.
  </p>
  <p>
    By the end, your Blume site sends page views and a few chosen reader
    actions to Google Analytics 4, your docs domain is verified in both search
    consoles, and you have a worksheet with a dated baseline to compare each
    month against.
  </p>
  <p>
    Blume's part is small: an analytics adapter, the events it already sends,
    and the verification tags. The rest happens in each tool. This guide uses
    GA4 because its channel rules already recognize AI assistants, but any
    adapter from the <a href="/docs/configuration/analytics">analytics docs</a>{" "}
    works if you swap the event call. If you only want a traffic trend, GA4's
    Traffic acquisition report answers that on its own. Nothing here measures
    how often an assistant mentions you without a link.
  </p>

  <h2>Citations, clicks, and activation are different numbers</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Measure</th>
        <th scope="col">What it tells you</th>
        <th scope="col">Where to read it</th>
        <th scope="col">What it can't tell you</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Citations</td>
        <td>An AI answer listed your page as a source</td>
        <td>Bing Webmaster Tools, AI Performance report</td>
        <td>Whether anyone clicked</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>AI impressions</td>
        <td>Google's AI Overviews or AI Mode showed a link to your page</td>
        <td>Search Console, Generative AI performance report</td>
        <td>Whether anyone clicked, or what they searched for</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Search clicks</td>
        <td>
          Readers clicked your page in Google Search, AI Overviews and AI Mode
          included
        </td>
        <td>Search Console, Performance report, Web</td>
        <td>Which of those clicks came from an AI feature</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Assistant referrals</td>
        <td>A reader clicked through from an assistant</td>
        <td>GA4, by session source</td>
        <td>Visits that arrive with no referrer and no tag</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Activation</td>
        <td>The reader then did something you care about</td>
        <td>GA4 key events and Blume's events</td>
        <td>Whether they went on to use the product</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Keep them in separate columns. A page that is cited often and clicked
    rarely may have answered the question in place, which is a different
    finding from a page that gets clicks and no follow-up.
  </p>

  <h2>Decide which reader actions count</h2>
  <p>
    A visit from an assistant tells you a reader arrived. Pick one or two
    actions that mean the page did its job. Blume already sends some of them
    as <a href="/docs/configuration/analytics#custom-events">analytics events</a>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>feedback</code>, with <code>helpful</code> set to{" "}
      <code>"yes"</code> or <code>"no"</code>, from the{" "}
      <a href="/docs/configuration#page-feedback">rating</a> at the end of
      every docs page. It's on by default.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>search_select</code>, when a reader{" "}
      <a href="/docs/configuration/search#search-analytics">searches</a> and
      opens a result.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>ask_answer</code>, when{" "}
      <a href="/docs/configuration/assistant#analytics">the assistant</a>{" "}
      finishes an answer, if you've turned it on.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For product docs, the strongest signal is usually a click into the product
    itself: a sign-up, a dashboard, an API keys page. Blume doesn't send that
    one, so add it with a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/analytics#custom-scripts"><code>script()</code></a>{" "}
    adapter. This config also sets the site URL and the two verification tags
    you'll need later:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`import { defineConfig } from "blume";
import { googleAnalytics, script } from "blume/analytics";

// Send an app_click event when a reader follows a link into the Acme app.
const appClicks = \`
(function () {
  if (window.acmeAppClicks) return;
  window.acmeAppClicks = true;
  document.addEventListener("click", function (event) {
    var link = event.target instanceof Element ? event.target.closest("a[href]") : null;
    if (!link || link.origin !== "https://app.acme.example") return;
    if (typeof window.gtag === "function") {
      window.gtag("event", "app_click", { link_url: link.href });
    }
  });
})();
\`;

export default defineConfig({
  deployment: { site: "https://docs.acme.example" },
  seo: {
    metatags: {
      "google-site-verification": "abc123",
      "msvalidate.01": "def456",
    },
  },
  analytics: [
    googleAnalytics({ id: "G-XXXXXXXXXX" }),
    script({ content: appClicks }),
  ],
});`}
    lang="ts"
    title="blume.config.ts"
  />
  <p>
    Change <code>https://app.acme.example</code> to your product's origin, and
    replace the measurement ID and both tokens with your own. Analytics loads in
    production builds only, so deploy before you look for events. With{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/consent"><code>consent</code></a> set, both
    tags wait until the reader allows analytics.
  </p>
  <p>Then tell GA4 what to count:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      In Admin, under Data display, click <strong>Events</strong>. Once{" "}
      <code>app_click</code> appears under <strong>Recent events</strong>,
      click the star next to it to mark it as a key event.
    </li>
    <li>
      In Admin, under Data display, open <strong>Custom definitions</strong>,
      click <strong>Create custom dimensions</strong>, and add one named{" "}
      <code>helpful</code> with the Event scope and the event parameter{" "}
      <code>helpful</code>. That splits <code>feedback</code> into yes and no.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Read the referral URLs you actually get</h2>
  <p>
    Before you trust a report, look at one real referral. Ask ChatGPT a
    question your docs answer. If it cites one of your pages, click the link
    and read the address bar:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code="https://docs.acme.example/quickstart?utm_source=chatgpt.com"
    lang="txt"
  />
  <p>
    OpenAI adds <code>utm_source=chatgpt.com</code> to referral links from
    ChatGPT, so GA4 records the session source as <code>chatgpt.com</code> even
    when the browser sends no referrer. Other assistants may send a referrer, a
    tag, or nothing. Try the ones your readers use and write down what you see.
  </p>
  <p>
    In GA4, open Reports, then Acquisition, then{" "}
    <strong>Traffic acquisition</strong>. Switch the table's dimension to{" "}
    <strong>Session source</strong> and search for each assistant's hostname,
    like <code>chatgpt.com</code>, <code>perplexity.ai</code>,{" "}
    <code>gemini.google.com</code>, <code>copilot.microsoft.com</code>, and{" "}
    <code>claude.ai</code>. Keep the ones that return rows. While{" "}
    <code>app_click</code> is your only key event, the table's Key events column
    is your activation count for each source.
  </p>
  <p>
    GA4's default channel group also has an <strong>AI Assistant</strong>{" "}
    channel, for sessions whose referrer matches Google's list of AI
    assistants. Google's examples are ChatGPT, Gemini, DeepSeek, Copilot, and
    Grok. Its Organic Search channel includes clicks from AI Overviews and AI
    Mode, so clicks from Google's own AI features land there, not under AI
    Assistant.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every AI visit is one of three kinds: tagged, referred, or neither. The
    third lands in Direct with nothing to say where it came from. Record the
    first two, and don't try to estimate the third.
  </p>

  <h2>Compare Google and Bing reports</h2>
  <p>
    Add your docs domain in Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools,
    choose the HTML meta tag method, and put each tool's token in{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/metadata#your-own-tags"
      ><code>seo.metatags</code></a
    >{" "}
    as the config above does. In Search Console, the meta tag works for a
    URL-prefix property; a Domain property needs a DNS record instead. Then
    submit <code>https://docs.acme.example/sitemap.xml</code> in both. Blume
    writes the <a href="/docs/discoverability/sitemap-and-robots#sitemap"
      >sitemap</a
    > on every build once <code>deployment.site</code> is set.
  </p>
  <h3>Search Console</h3>
  <p>
    Google reports AI Overviews and AI Mode in two places. The Performance
    report's Web search type counts them together with the rest of Search: a
    click on a link in either counts as a click, and every link in an AI
    Overview shares that overview's single position. Treat the Web totals as
    all of Google Search, AI included. The Pages tab gives you clicks per page.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>Generative AI performance report</strong>, listed with the
    other performance reports, takes the AI features' share of that Web data
    and counts impressions only: how many times a link to your site was shown in
    AI Overviews or AI Mode, with two of your links in the same answer counting
    once. It has Pages, Countries, Devices, and Dates tabs, no queries, and no
    clicks, and it leaves out experiments in Search Labs. Keep its impressions
    in their own column. Dividing Web clicks by them gives no click-through
    rate, since those clicks include ordinary results.
  </p>
  <h3>Bing Webmaster Tools</h3>
  <p>
    The AI Performance report, in public preview, counts citations across
    Microsoft Copilot, AI-generated summaries in Bing, and select partner
    integrations. It shows total citations, average cited pages per day, the
    grounding queries behind them, and citations per page, plus newer previews
    of intents, topics, citation share, and a compare view. Bing says the
    grounding queries are a sample of overall citation activity, and that the
    counts reflect how often pages are cited, not ranking or placement. None of
    its numbers are clicks.
  </p>
  <h3>Line them up by page</h3>
  <p>
    Put citations from Bing, AI impressions and clicks from Search Console,
    and AI-referred sessions and key events from GA4 side by side for each
    page. Read the pairs,
    not the totals:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cited often, few referrals.</strong> The answer may have been
      enough. That isn't a failure to fix.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Referrals, no actions.</strong> The page may not deliver what the
      answer promised. Read it as someone arriving from that answer would.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Actions rising.</strong> Still not adoption. That shows up in your
      product's own data, like new API keys that make a first request.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Keep a dated baseline</h2>
  <p>
    These numbers only mean something next to earlier ones taken the same way.
    Once a month, read every tool over the same range (the previous calendar
    month) and add a row per number to one file:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`date,range,source,measure,scope,value,notes
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,Bing AI Performance,Total citations,site,,
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,Bing AI Performance,Average cited pages,site,,
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,Bing AI Performance,Citations,/quickstart,,
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,Search Console Generative AI,Impressions,site,,
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,Search Console Generative AI,Impressions,/quickstart,,
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,Search Console Web,Clicks,site,,includes AI Overviews and AI Mode
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,Search Console Web,Clicks,/quickstart,,
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,GA4,Sessions,Session source chatgpt.com,,
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,GA4,Sessions,AI Assistant channel,,
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,GA4,Key events (app_click),Session source chatgpt.com,,
2026-10-01,2026-09-01 to 2026-09-30,GA4,feedback helpful=yes,site,,
2026-09-18,,Change,Rewrote the quickstart,/quickstart,,shipped to production`}
    lang="csv"
    title="ai-search-baseline.csv"
  />
  <p>
    The dates are examples; use the day you read the numbers. Add a row per
    page you care about, and a row per assistant hostname you found. Log your
    own changes as dated rows too, like the rewritten quickstart above, so a
    jump has a likely cause.
  </p>
  <p>
    Log the tools' changes as well. Bing's report is a preview, and GA4 matches
    the AI Assistant channel against its own list of assistants, so a number
    can move with nothing changing on your site. Never compare a partial month
    with a full one.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <h3>GA4 shows no events from the docs</h3>
  <p>
    Blume adds analytics to production builds only, so pages from{" "}
    <code>blume dev</code> send nothing. Check that the deployed page's{" "}
    <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> has the <code>gtag</code> scripts. With{" "}
    <code>consent</code> set, nothing loads until a reader allows analytics, so
    every count covers consenting readers only, and the page rating is hidden
    from everyone else.
  </p>
  <h3>ChatGPT sessions are missing from the AI Assistant channel</h3>
  <p>
    GA4 assigns that channel from the referrer, while ChatGPT's tag sets the
    session source. A tagged session that arrives without a referrer can land
    in another channel. Count by Session source, and use the channel as a
    cross-check.
  </p>
  <h3>The helpful dimension is empty</h3>
  <p>
    GA4 makes a new custom dimension available 24 to 48 hours after it's
    created and events carrying the parameter have arrived. Rate a page on the
    live site yourself, then check again two days later.
  </p>
  <h3>Bing's AI Performance report is empty</h3>
  <p>
    Check that the site is verified and the sitemap was read. The report only
    counts citations that happened, so an empty range can mean Bing's AI
    answers cited none of your pages, not that setup failed. Blume's{" "}
    <code>robots.txt</code> allows every crawler, so if Bingbot still can't
    fetch your pages, look at your host's or CDN's bot rules, as{" "}
    <a href="/guides/ai-search-documentation-crawlability"
      >checking AI crawler access</a
    > covers.
  </p>
  <h3>Agent reads never show up</h3>
  <p>
    Agents that read your{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/llms-txt"><code>llms.txt</code></a>, a
    page's <a href="/docs/discoverability/markdown">Markdown</a>, or your{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/mcp">MCP server</a> never run the analytics
    script, so no client-side tool counts them. Only your host's request logs
    see those reads.
  </p>
  <h3>Search Console shows no clicks for a page</h3>
  <p>
    To appear as a supporting link in AI Overviews or AI Mode, a page must be
    indexed and eligible to show with a snippet, the same as a regular result.
    If it isn't indexed, work through{" "}
    <a href="/guides/documentation-not-indexed">pages that are not indexed</a>{" "}
    first.
  </p>
</GuidePage>
